Hi Igor,

> One honest gap: I could not yet take the voltage-vs-frequency
> curve (my devfreq module holds the NPU rail at 850 mV as a
> regulator consumer, so undervolting from a second consumer is
> refused). Future work.

I took one on RK3576 this week, from the correctness side rather than
the throughput side, and it answered something I had been chasing for
four days.

Same wall as yours on the rail: a second consumer is refused. So the
curve is one device tree per point, flashed and rebooted.

The setting first. Mainline sets no rate for CLK_RKNN_DSU0, which
clocks both NPU cores and the convolution buffer they share, and no
board sets vdd_npu_s0 either, so my ROCK 4D runs the NPU at
786.432 MHz on the 750 mV its PMIC comes up with. Rockchip's own OPP
table for this NPU asks 800 mV of its 800 MHz step at the worst
leakage bin, and 725 mV only at 600.

One core is exact at that setting. Two cores with jobs in flight at
the same time are not: the second one writes single words of its
output wrong, always the same position in the array, and the wrong
value is the right value plus a small integer. Of 64 wrong words I
took apart, 63 differed from the correct one by an integer to within
a twentieth, and 25 by exactly 1024, so it reads as a bit of the
accumulator rather than as anything the software could have computed.
Re-reading the same word after invalidating the CPU's cache of the
buffer gave the same wrong value 48 times out of 48, so the hardware
wrote it.

The curve. Four device trees, same board, kernel and userspace. A
pass is 5400 rows of a batched matrix multiply, each row compared
with the same multiply done one row at a time:

  786 MHz, 750 mV    11 to 25 wrong rows a pass
  594 MHz, 750 mV    0, 0, 0, 0
  786 MHz, 800 mV    0, 0, 0, 0
  786 MHz, 850 mV    0, 0, 0, 0

50 mV at one rate is the whole difference between exact and wrong,
and 800 mV is the vendor's own number for that step.

Two things in it for your series.

The first is the OPP question you put to Tomeu, whether the table
should stop at 600 rather than follow the vendor range. From here the
column that must not be dropped is the voltage one. A rate without
its voltage is what mainline has today, and it is what corrupts.

The second is a method note, and I raise it because it would have
fooled me. Your numbers are one inference thread pinned to one A76
with a bit-exact oracle, which is the harness I would have built, and
on RK3576 a single-threaded harness cannot see this fault at all: it
needs two cores with work in flight at the same time. If your 900 and
1000 MHz rows were taken with one NPU core busy, they may not survive
three of them at 850 mV. One run with all three loaded and the oracle
on would settle it, and it is cheap next to what a wrong OPP table
costs later.

On the SCMI side, an addition to what I told you in August. I said
routing the RK3576 NPU clock through SCMI gave zero completed jobs
and 83 scheduler timeouts. I now know one thing not to do: an
assigned-clock-rates on the SCMI clock in the NPU node hangs the
board before the console comes up, with no kernel output at all. I
have not proven why. What I can point at is that the vendor's driver
never sets that rate from DT either. rockchip_opp_config_clks()
returns early when the clock is an SCMI clock and the device is not
runtime active, so the rate is only ever set from the runtime resume
path with the domains already on. Your EL3 telemetry is a read, so
none of this touches it, but if you end up writing the SCMI rate from
a notifier the ordering will matter.

And a correction to my own August mail in this thread. I said our
DSU0 "sits at 594 to 786 MHz permanently". It is 786.432 MHz with
nothing set. 594 is what my v12 now assigns in the DT, for the reason
above.

Cheers,
Jiaxing
